Title
-----

OCG - Overlapping Class Generator

Authors
-------
Alain Guenoche (I2M, guenoche@iml.univ-mrs.fr) with contributions from Benoit Robisson and Charles E. Chapple  

Maintener
---------
Lionel Spinelli (TAGC, lionel.spinelli@univ-amu.fr)

Separate OpenAI variant
-----------------------

OCG_fast_openai.c is a separate optimized variant. OCG.c, OCG_fast.c and
their existing executables are preserved. Build with GCC and OpenMP:

    gcc -O3 -fopenmp ocg/OCG_fast_openai.c -o ocg/bin/ocg_fast_openai

On Windows, use ocg/bin/ocg_fast_openai.exe as the output filename. The
compiler's bin directory must be on PATH. Omit -fopenmp for a serial build;
do not use -ffast-math, which can change modularity comparisons.

The Python runner uses the OpenAI executable and supports worker control:

    python -m pipelines.run_ocg_c --input data/processed_networks/HuRI.parquet --threads 4

The default requests up to eight workers. Small merge workloads run serially
to avoid thread overhead. More workers do not always improve runtime; compare
--threads 1 and --threads 4 for the selected network. The binary fingerprint
keeps previous results separate from runs of the new implementation.

Changes relative to OCG_fast.c:
- Hash vertex labels during collection instead of scanning all previous labels.
- Transform weights into modularity values in place, removing an 8*N*N-byte
  dense matrix from peak storage (1.8 GB at 15,000 vertices).
- Make OpenMP inner-loop counters private to prevent data races.
- Initialize the modularity diagonal read by the final contribution filter.
- Build a compact list of eligible merge updates and start OpenMP workers only
  when at least 32 updates and 100,000 protein-pair evaluations are available.
  Row-cache maintenance stays serial below 2,048 active classes.

The algorithm still uses quadratic dense storage, and its hierarchical merge
decisions remain sequential. Maximal-clique and edge initialization can be
expensive. The Python runner retains the 30,000-node / 500,000-edge limits;
these limits do not guarantee that a graph fits available memory.

Validation: native tests compile serial and OpenMP versions and compare exact
partition output with one and four threads, including all initialization modes,
class-size constraints and weighted input. Set CC to GCC and run:

    python -m pytest tests/test_ocg_c.py tests/test_run_ocg_c.py

Details
-------
This program builds an overlapping class system from an unweighted simple graph G=(V,E).
 Let |V|=n and |E|=m.										   
  												   
 It is essentially a hierarchical ascending algorithm joining two classes at each step.	   
 The optimized criterion is the modularity. It can be either the average gain or the total gain. 
  												   
 The initial overlapping class system can be : 						   
 - the set of all maximal cliques (it can take a long time to establish)       		   
 - the set of edges (many initial classes (m) implying many steps (O(m))			   
 - the set of "centered cliques" (at most n), giving a fast solution for large graphs.	   
  												   
 Two class systems can be calculated, the one maximazing the modularity, or the final one.	   
 In that case, the expected minimum number of clusters and the maximum caldinality of the 	   
 final clusters are required.									   
  												   
 Fusion of classes are realized until one of these conditions is fullfiled. 			   
 When no more class fusion can be realized the algorithm stops.				   
  												   
 Let p be the number of initial classes. The complexity is this algorithm is O(p^3).		   
 
